<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01AV">Warning <span data-tag="01AV">11.10.7.6</span>.</a> In the situation of Definition <a data-tag="01AQ" href="/tag/01AQ">11.10.7.1</a>, we will often abuse terminology by referring to the morphism $f: X \rightarrow Y$ in the slice category $(\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }})_{/S}$ as a <em>covariant equivalence</em> (<em>contravariant equivalence</em>) if it is a covariant equivalence relative to $S$. Beware that there is some danger of confusion, because the notion of covariant equivalence depends on $S$. For example, the inclusion map $\{ 1\}  \hookrightarrow \Delta ^1$ is not a covariant equivalence relative to $\Delta ^1$ (Warning <a data-tag="01AU" href="/tag/01AU">11.10.7.5</a>), but <em>is</em> a covariant equivalence relative to the final object $\Delta ^{0} \in \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}$ (Example <a data-tag="01AT" href="/tag/01AT">11.10.7.4</a>). </p>
